365: actual tangent splitting is
366: \[
367:  TW_{\rm rel}|_{\rm locally\ free}
368:                     \cong\pi^*TZ\oplus\mathbb R_\delta .      \tag{IE.9}
369: \]
370: Choose its connection to match the actual
371: boundary tangent connections after a collar
372: interpolation. The basic Chern--Weil form
373: \[
374:                       \Omega=\frac{4x^3-p_1(TZ)x}{24}         \tag{IE.10}
375: \]
376: is closed. Integrate each finite chart as
377: \(d^{-1}\int_{\widetilde U}\). Ordinary Stokes
378: on the finite covers and their averaged
379: partition of unity cancels every interior
380: face with exactly these multiplicities.
381: The finite-isotropy strata are not boundary
382: faces. Consequently
383: \(\int_{\partial Z}\Omega=0\).
384: 
385: On a fixed normal link, (IE.9) restricts
386: to the fixed tangent and weighted normal
387: bundles. Its equivariant Thom denominator
388: is \(\prod_i(x_i+w_i u)\). The finite
389: chart integral, equivalently the weighted
390: projective power map, gives
391: \(1/\prod_iw_i\), with every negative
392: normal conjugation's orientation sign.
393: Thus its actual contribution is (SZ.57).
394: The compact interior/H values and (IE.8)
395: are exactly the terms in this Stokes
396: identity, including finite stabilizers.
397: No spin structure on this comparison
398: bordism or eight-dimensional signature
399: formula is used.
400: 
401: ## IE.5. Outer boundary and Jin conventions
402: 
403: Orient \(W_{\rm c}\) complexly and the
404: reference piece oppositely. Define
405: \(\delta\wedge\operatorname{or}(Z)
406: =\operatorname{or}(W_{\rm rel})\).
407: Near the outer parameter boundary,
408: \[
409:  \operatorname{or}(W)
410:  =\delta\wedge\operatorname{or}(Y)
411:                   \wedge d\rho\wedge d\theta .
412: \]
413: Since \(\dim Y=5\), its quotient boundary
414: orientation is
415: \[
416:                          -\operatorname{or}(Y)\wedge d\theta.       \tag{IE.11}
417: \]
418: At an inner fixed-normal boundary the
419: minus sign from removing the normal disc
420: and the minus sign from quotienting an
421: oriented boundary by the odd-dimensional
422: orbit cancel. Its orientation is the
423: positive complex-normal link convention
424: of (SZ.57). Therefore Stokes gives
425: \[
426:                  A_{\rm physical,difference}
427:                           =\sum_F\mathcal R(F).          \tag{IE.12}
428: \]
429: 
430: Jin identifies \((z,0)\sim(f(z),1)\).
431: The positive physical family with return
432: \(f\) has the inverse parameter marking.
433: The map to the Jin six-manifold reverses
434: that circle and preserves the physical
435: Euler line. Hence
436: \[
437:                   A_{\rm Jin,difference}
438:                            =-\sum_F\mathcal R(F).         \tag{IE.13}
439: \]
440: The common conifold replacement in IE.2
441: uses the same transported positive
442: meridian, so adds no line or orientation
443: change.
444: 
445: ## IE.6. Scalar-order independence and exact local values
446: 
447: Over \(U\) the ordinary suspension, with
448: no linearization zero, is an actual family
449: over the product of a source annulus and
450: a small scalar annulus:
451: \[
452:                    B_\lambda=2(-1)^{b+1}\lambda q_*U_a^b.
453: \]
454: There is no finite cap in this local
455: family, so every integer scalar winding
456: is a closed local loop. Its source-base
457: boundary family extends over the source
458: disc, with the actual periods and Cartier
459: frames. Choose IE.1's transport on this
460: entire product.
461: 
462: A pair of pants mapping into this
463: product realizes the based-loop identity
464: \[
465:  (\epsilon\text{ turn},N\text{ scalar turns})
466:    =(\epsilon\text{ turn},0)+(0,N\text{ scalar turns}).    \tag{IE.14}
467: \]
468: Pull back the actual local family and
469: quotient its free physical circle.
470: This is a seven-dimensional bordism
471: of the mapping-torus pieces. Glue the
472: source-base boundary collars by the
473: full-product transport. The fixed-source
474: scalar-loop boundary is exactly the
475: reference piece of IE.1 and cancels.
476: Thus the closed relative collision
477: boundaries \(Q_{\rm rel}(N)\) and
478: \(Q_{\rm rel}(0)\) are bordant with
479: their actual Euler line. Their
480: characteristic difference is independent
481: of \(N\). This is a literal parameter
482: product and collar construction; no
483: division of a covered sphere class occurs.
484: 
485: For any admitted cap, its exterior
486: source/root/line family extends over
487: the source disc. IE.2 cancels it with
488: the constant exterior. Hence this
489: same local relative boundary represents
490: the fixed-scalar source loop for that
491: completed family. It suffices to use
492: the two fully executed orders \(N=8\)
493: for \((a,b)=(1,2)\), and \(N=6\) for
494: \((a,b)=(2,1)\):
495: \[
496:  \begin{array}{c|rrr}
497:  (a,b,N)&\text{interior/H}&N\text{ E doubles}&\text{total}\\ \hline
498:  (1,2,8)&2&-2&0\\
499:  (2,1,6)&5&-6&-1 .
500:  \end{array}                                           \tag{IE.15}
501: \]
502: Independent executions at \(N=4,12\)
503: in the first case and \(N=3,9\) in
504: the second give the same relative
505: sums. They check the arithmetic;
506: the independence proof is (IE.14).
507: 
508: By (IE.13), the proposed fixed-scalar
509: marked infinity Jin differences are
510: respectively \(0\) and \(+1\).
511: After the complete-map audit the
512: proved Jin-to-\(\Theta_7\) transfer
513: applies to the two actual completed
514: boundary mapping tori. The old
515: \(v\)/new \(\epsilon\) scalar path,
516: \(D^{-4}\) root endpoint and
517: half-loop fan map remain the explicit
518: dictionary of IF.1--6. Their winding
519: corrections are not absorbed into
520: (IE.15).
